  "query_preview": ".. \n\nTropical moisture in place across the forecast area will continue\nto fuel widespread showers and thunderstorms through this \nevening, as an upstream low along the California coast makes its \nway through the Great Basin. Ample deep layer shear will maintain \na favorable environment for organized convection including \nsupercells anywhere sufficient breaks in the cloud cover allow for\ndestabilization, as has been evidenced across southwest Utah over\nthe past few hours. Efficient rainfall rates will also continue \nto result in excessive runoff. Most large drainages across south \ncentral into east central Utah are already running high and fast, \nand as such Flood Warnings are in effect for these areas. \n\nA shortwave trough rounding the base of the upstream low will lift\nacross the forecast area this evening, focusing the heavier\nprecipitation further north into northern Utah/southwest Wyoming.\nInitially a band of showers and thunderstorms, potentially strong\nto severe, is expected to propagate across northwest Utah reaching\nthe Wasatch Front this evening, followed by a fairly broad\nstratiform region of precipitation associated with the passage of\nthe upper trough. Snow levels will fall to roughly 8000 feet\novernight, with accumulating snow largely above 9000 feet.\nPrevious forecast totals of 1-4\" with locally higher amounts still\nlook reasonable, with a few locally higher totals if precip is\nable to linger into Friday morning. \n\nAs the initial low ejects into the northern Rockies, a secondary\nupper low is forecast to form over the Desert Southwest Saturday,\nthen slowly translate into the central/southern Rockies Sunday.\nThis will maintain cool temperatures across the forecast area,\nalong with a chance of precipitation mainly across the eastern\nthird of the CWA. An upper trough across the Pacific Northwest\nlooks to keep temperatures near to slightly below climo for much\nof next week.",
